          "name": "A. Carbone",
          "authorId": "2060447692"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2024,
      "abstract": "\nThe paper focuses on the relationship between language and thought as presented by J. G. Fichte in the Jena Lectures on Logic and Metaphysics.\nHe proposes a genetic theory of language, building on the analysis of knowledge achieved through transcendental logic.\nLanguage is revealed as an instrument of knowledge: the sign is a linguistic image, natural and thetic rather than arbitrary, through which the ego marks and keeps clean the conscious intuitions of the self in order to remember them in place of a synthesis with a new schema produced by the imagination.\nThe Einbildungskraft, working both transcendently and sensibly, creates both schemata and signs, and acts as a memory: it thus builds up spoken languages. Languages are nothing more than the signature of the temporality of conscience, of the history of life expressed through them and still open to the possibility of enrichment.\nThe lessons on Logic and Metaphysics in Jena and the theory of language presented here bear witness to the influence of classical rhetoric, which Fichte studied at the Stift of Pforta (in fact he mentions Simonide of Ceo in his notes). They also demonstrate the Rammenau-born philosopher\u2019s intention to include a reflection on language in his system, which had long been a desideratum of researchers in the field.",
